{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user (via the cobbler orchestrator) has assigned task #5530: implement `cmd/sync/main.go` — a Go reimplementation of GNU sync that flushes filesystem caches. The task requires implementing four requirements from srd085-sync (R1.1-R1.4) covering: no-argument sync(2), per-file fsync(2), --data fdatasync(2), and --file-system syncfs(2). The implementation must follow the project's execution constitution, go style constitution, and match patterns from existing cmd/ utilities. Git operations are forbidden — the orchestrator handles them externally.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - GNU sync utility reimplementation in Go\n   - System calls: sync(2), fsync(2), fdatasync(2), syncfs(2)\n   - SIGPIPE handling via `pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()`\n   - Differential testing against `gsync` (Homebrew GNU coreutils)\n   - Manual flag parsing (not cobra — cmd/ utilities parse flags manually)\n   - `golang.org/x/sys/unix` for platform-specific syscalls (Syncfs)\n   - `syscall` package for Sync() and Fsync()\n   - Darwin/macOS platform (current build target)\n   - File descriptor operations: os.Open → fd → syscall\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd085-sync.yaml` (READ)\n     - Source of truth for requirements R1.1-R1.4 and R2.1-R2.3\n     - Key requirements:\n       - R1.1: No args → sync(2) flushes all caches\n       - R1.2: FILE args → fsync(2) on each file\n       - R1.3: `-d`/`--data` → fdatasync(2), exit 1 if no FILE\n       - R1.4: `-f`/`--file-system` → syncfs(2) for filesystem, exit 1 if no FILE\n       - R2.1: Exit 0 on success\n       - R2.2: Exit 1 on failure\n       - R2.3: SIGPIPE handling via pkg/sys\n     - Non-goals: no filesystem-type-specific sync semantics\n\n   - `cmd/touch/main.go` (READ — reference pattern, 366 lines)\n     - Shows canonical cmd/ utility structure: copyright header, options struct, main() with InstallSIGPIPEHandler(), parseArgs(), run()\n     - Manual flag parsing with short and long flags, `--` end-of-flags\n     - Error format: `fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"touch: %s\\n\", err)`\n     - Uses `golang.org/x/sys/unix` and `github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys`\n     - Pattern: `os.Exit(run(opts, files))` from main\n\n   - `cmd/sync/main.go` (TO CREATE)\n     - Must implement sync utility with flag parsing for -d/--data and -f/--file-system\n     - Design decisions D1-D5 specify: InstallSIGPIPEHandler, syscall.Sync(), os.Open+defer Close+syscall calls, unix.Syncfs, pkg/sys import\n\n   - `pkg/sys/sigpipe.go` (provided in prompt context)\n     - Contains `InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` function\n     - Creates buffered channel, signal.Notify for SIGPIPE, goroutine exits 0\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No errors encountered yet. The task was in the research/planning phase when interrupted.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Read the SRD to understand exact requirements for sync modes and exit codes\n   - Read cmd/touch/main.go as the reference pattern for how cmd/ utilities are structured (manual arg parsing, error formatting, main→run pattern)\n   - Platform consideration: macOS may not have syncfs(2) or fdatasync(2) in the standard syscall package, requiring golang.org/x/sys/unix. The design decisions explicitly call for unix.Syncfs.\n   - The -d and -f flags both require FILE arguments and must exit 1 if none provided\n   - When -d and -f are not specified but files are given, default is fsync(2) per R1.2\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - Message 1: The complete task prompt from the cobbler orchestrator containing the full project context, task description (#5530), requirements (srd085 R1.1-R1.4), design decisions (D1-D5), acceptance criteria (AC1-AC6), execution constitution, go style constitution, constraints, and all reference source code/documentation inline.\n   - Message 2: \"CRITICAL: Respond with TEXT ONLY. Do NOT call any tools.\" — requesting a detailed summary of the conversation.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Create `cmd/sync/main.go` implementing srd085 R1.1-R1.4\n   - Verify compilation with `go build -o bin/sync ./cmd/sync/`\n   - Verify with `go vet ./cmd/sync/`\n   - Test basic functionality (AC3-AC6)\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I had just finished reading the two required reference files (`srd085-sync.yaml` and `cmd/touch/main.go`) and was about to write the `cmd/sync/main.go` file. No code had been written yet. I was in the planning phase, having gathered:\n   - The exact requirements from the SRD (sync modes, exit codes, SIGPIPE)\n   - The canonical pattern from cmd/touch/main.go (copyright header, options struct, manual flag parsing, main→parseArgs→run flow)\n   - The design decisions specifying which syscalls to use (syscall.Sync, syscall.Fsync, syscall.Fdatasync/unix.Fdatasync, unix.Syncfs)\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Write `cmd/sync/main.go` following the touch pattern with:\n   - Copyright header\n   - Options struct with `data bool` and `fileSystem bool` fields\n   - Manual flag parsing for `-d`/`--data` and `-f`/`--file-system` and `--`\n   - main() calling sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler(), parseArgs(), os.Exit(run())\n   - run() that: if no files and no flags → syscall.Sync(); if files with no flags → fsync each; if --data → fdatasync each; if --file-system → syncfs each; if --data or --file-system with no files → exit 1\n   - Then verify with `go build -o bin/sync ./cmd/sync/` and `go vet ./cmd/sync/`\n\n   This directly corresponds to the task: \"Implement cmd/sync core (srd085 R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.4)\" with the file to create being `cmd/sync/main.go`.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/pdiddy/.claude/projects/-privat