{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LzW39H68aKUPZ7xBSVAzxB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting! On macOS:\n- `gsync -d` with no file → error \"gsync: --data needs at least one argument\", exit 1\n- `gsync -f` with no file → exit 0, no error\n\nWait, the SRD says R1.4: \"Must exit 1 if no FILE is given with -f.\" But gsync exits 0 with -f and no file. Let me check again...\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the output: `gsync -f` with no file gives exit 0. But the SRD says it must exit 1. Hmm, but the task says \"match gsync\" and the SRD. Let me re-read...\n \nSo there's a mismatch here—the SRD requires exit 1 when -f is given without files, but GNU sync actually falls back to a global sync() call when no files are provided, which succeeds with exit 0. I need to figure out which behavior to implement. gsync. But the task requirements also specify these SRD items... Let me verify what gsync actually does in this scenario.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":618,"cache_read_input_tokens":28661,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":618},"output_tokens":36,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"32d25cb8-d0bd-458b-866d-1aa82465e655","uuid":"593bbb1b-55c7-45fd-ba86-2a7435efa5a1"}
