{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Epfqg1uro6xbjK9RY74f6N","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So the actual gsync behavior is:\n- `gsync -f` with no file → exit 0 (just does sync(), the global sync)\n- `gsync -d` with no file → error \"gsync: --data needs at least one argument\", exit 1\n- `gsync /nonexistent/file` → error \"gsync: error opening '/nonexistent/file': No such file or directory\", exit 1\n- `gsync --nonexistent` → error with \"unrecognized option\" + \"Try 'gsync --help'\", exit 1\n\nThe SRD says R1.4 must exit 1 if no FILE with -f, but gsync doesn't do that. \n\nSince differential testing is the acceptance criterion, I need to match gsync's actual behavior rather than the stated requirements—so `-f` with no files should succeed with exit 0, while `-d` with no files should error and exit 1.\n\nFor the macOS implementation, I'm mapping the sync operations to their syscall equivalents: `sync()` to `syscall.Sync()`, `fsync(fd)` to `syscall.Fsync(fd)`, and since macOS doesn't have `fdatasync`, gsync likely falls back to `fsync` for that case.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":974,"cache_read_input_tokens":29279,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":974},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"32d25cb8-d0bd-458b-866d-1aa82465e655","uuid":"946c5364-4bc3-43c1-82b4-b6010f57dc37"}
