{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GpVfZWFzePiYGAXBvnptA3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The issue is case sensitivity: Go's `syscall.ENOENT.Error()` returns \"no such file or directory\" (lowercase \"n\") while gsync uses \"No such file or directory\" (uppercase \"N\"). This is because gsync uses `strerror()` from libc which capitalizes the first letter, while Go's errno strings are lowercase.\n\nLet me check how other cmd/ utilities in this repo handle this. Looking at the touch code... `sysMsg` just returns `pe.Err.Error()` which is the Go lowercase version. Does the touch test pass with lowercase errors?\n\n\nI need to understand how the test framework handles error message comparison. The diff tests will fail if the capitalization doesn't match, so I should look at whether testutils does any normalization or if other utilities in the repo have already solved this problem with their own error messages.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":128,"cache_read_input_tokens":40877,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":128},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"32d25cb8-d0bd-458b-866d-1aa82465e655","uuid":"474db4bd-8929-48eb-94cf-538609927451"}
