{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PMakbXz1Vd38bWLNQWXTj6","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have a comprehensive understanding. Let me create a summary document with the findings:\n\n## Summary of NormalizeFunc Usage in go-unix-utils Tests\n\nBased on my analysis, I found how **NormalizeFunc** is used to handle binary name differences in stderr across test files. Here's what I discovered:\n\n### Definition (Location: `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5530/pkg/testutils/difftest.go`)\n\n```go\n// NormalizeFunc transforms raw output bytes before comparison.\n// R1.4: named type alias for normalization functions.\ntype NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte\n```\n\nNormalizeFunc is a function type that processes byte slices. It's applied to both stdout and stderr of test binaries before comparison to handle differences that are not semantically important (like the binary name in error messages).\n\n---\n\n### Usage Pattern 1: Discard Stderr (Simple Approach)\n\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5530/cmd/md5sum/md5sum_test.go` (lines 39, 114-268)\n\n```go\ndiscardStderr := testutils.NormalizeFunc(func([]byte) []byte { return nil })\n\ntests := []testutils.DiffTest{\n    // R1.4: missing file exits 1, prints error to stderr\n    {\n        Name:      \"missing-file\",\n        Args:      []string{filepath.Join(tmpDir, \"nonexistent.txt\")},\n        ExitCode:  1,\n        Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{discardStderr},\n    },\n    // R1.4: missing file among valid files continues processing\n    {\n        Name:      \"missing-among-valid\",\n        Args:      []string{helloFile, filepath.Join(tmpDir, \"nonexistent.txt\"), emptyFile},\n        ExitCode:  1,\n        Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{discardStderr},\n    },\n    // ... more test cases using discardStderr\n}\n```\n\n**Pattern:** Creates a single `discardStderr` function that replaces all stderr with `nil` (empty output), then applies it to multiple test cases. This handles cases where error messages contain the binary name (like \"gmd5sum\" vs \"md5sum\").\n\n---\n\n### Usage Pattern 2: Regex-based Normalization (Sophisticated Approach)\n\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5530/cmd/sort/sort_test.go` (lines 16-23, 185-236)\n\n```go\nvar progRe = regexp.MustCompile(`(?m)^(\\S*/)?g?sort:`)\nvar tryRe = regexp.MustCompile(`'(\\S*/)?g?sort --help'`)\n\nfunc normStderr(b []byte) []byte {\n    b = progRe.ReplaceAll(b, []byte(\"PROG:\"))\n    b = tryRe.ReplaceAll(b, []byte(\"'PROG --help'\"))\n    return b\n}\n\ntests := []testutils.DiffTest{\n    {\n        Name:      \"check_unsorted\",\n        Args:      []string{\"-c\"},\n        Stdin:     []byte(\"b\\na\\nc\\n\"),\n        Env:       env,\n        Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{normStderr},\n    },\n    {\n        Name:      \"check_numeric_unsorted\",\n        Args:      []string{\"-cn\"},\n        Stdin:     []byte(\"1\\n10\\n2\\n\"),\n        Env:       env,\n        Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{normStderr},\n    },\n    {\n        Name:      \"check_unique_fail\",\n        Args:      []string{\"-cu\"},\n        Stdin:     []byte(\"a\\nb\\nb\\nc\\n\"),\n        Env:       env,\n        Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{normStderr},\n    },\n}\n```\n\n**Pattern:** Uses regex to normalize the binary name in error messages. The regex patterns handle:\n- Optional path prefix (`\\S*/`)?\n- Optional 'g' prefix to match both `sort` and `gsort` \n- Replaces them with a placeholder (`PROG:`)\n\nThis allows the test to compare error messages where the only difference is the binary name prefix.\n\n---\n\n### Usage Pattern 3: Simple Inline Normalizers\n\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5530/cmd/head/head_test.go` (lines 23, 147-268)\n\n```go\ndiscardStderr := testutils.NormalizeFunc(func([]byte) []byte { return nil })\n\ntests := []testutils.DiffTest{\n    // Error: invalid byte count\n    {\n        Name:      \"bytes-invalid\",\n        Args:      []string{\"-c\", \"abc\"},\n        ExitCode:  1,\n        Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{discardStderr},\n    },\n    // ... more test cases\n    {\n        Name:      \"nonexistent-with-valid\",\n        Args:      []string{\"nonexistent.txt\", \"a.txt\"},\n        WorkDir:   dir,\n        ExitCode:  1,\n        Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{discardStderr},\n    },\n}\n```\n\n**Pattern:** Same as Pattern 1 - simple discard pattern used for error cases where the binary name difference is expected.\n\n---\n\n### How It Works in the Test Framework\n\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5530/pkg/testutils/difftest.go` (lines 168-174)\n\n```go\nfunc applyNormalizers(res binaryResult, fns []NormalizeFunc) binaryResult {\n    for _, fn := range fns {\n        res.stdout = fn(res.stdout)\n        res.stderr = fn(res.stderr)\n    }\n    return res\n}\n```\n\nThe framework applies normalizers to **both** the Go binary and reference binary (GNU) outputs before comparison. This ensures both outputs are normalized identically before the assertion.\n\n---\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n1. **Simple approach**: Use `NormalizeFunc(func([]byte) []byte { return nil })` to discard stderr entirely when the only difference is the binary name.\n\n2. **Sophisticated approach**: Use regex-based normalization to replace the variable parts (binary name) with placeholders, preserving the rest of the error message for comparison.\n\n3. **Composability**: Multiple `NormalizeFunc` functions can be applied in order via the `Normalize` slice in `DiffTest`.\n\n4. **Purpose**: Handles the fundamental difference that the Go binary is named \"gcat\", \"gmd5sum\", etc., while GNU tools are named \"cat\", \"md5sum\", etc., so error messages will differ in their program name prefix."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"32d25cb8-d0bd-458b-866d-1aa82465e655","uuid":"09c69880-9b04-482d-91d4-8e537625709b","timestamp":"2026-05-23T12:45:30.796Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5530, find a cmd/*_test.go file that includes error-producing test cases (where stderr output differs because of the binary name like \"gcat\" vs \"cat\"). I want to see how the NormalizeFunc is used to handle the binary name difference in stderr. Show me the relevant normalize function usage from 2-3 test files. Search breadth: medium.","agentId":"a56418db93796d437","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have a comprehensive understanding. Let me create a summary document with the findings:\n\n## Summary of NormalizeFunc Usage in go-unix-utils Tests\n\nBased on my analysis, I found how **NormalizeFunc** is used to handle binary name differences in stderr across test files. Here's what I discovered:\n\n### Definition (Location: `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5530/pkg/testutils/difftest.go`)\n\n```go\n// NormalizeFunc transforms raw output bytes before comparison.\n// R1.4: named type alias for normalization functions.\ntype NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte\n```\n\nNormalizeFunc is a function type that processes byte slices. It's applied to both stdout and stderr of test binaries before comparison to handle differences that are not semantically important (like the binary name in error messages).\n\n---\n\n### Usage Pattern 1: Discard Stderr (Simple Approach)\n\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5530/cmd/md5sum/md5sum_test.go` (lines 39, 114-268)\n\n```go\ndiscardStderr := testutils.NormalizeFunc(func([]byte) []byte { return nil })\n