{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012C9FqcEnJrRkht3uHJBSX4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the pattern. For the sync test, I need to use a normalizer. Two approaches:\n1. `discardStderr` — simple but loses error message validation  \n2. Regex-based normalization — replaces binary name with placeholder\n\nThe `sort` test uses the regex approach which is better for validating error message content. Let me use that pattern.\n\nBut first, let me also fix the error message capitalization issue. On macOS, Go's `syscall.ENOENT.Error()` returns \"no such file or directory\" (lowercase), while the C library's `strerror()` returns \"No such file or directory\" (uppercase). \n\nThe diff test framework runs both binaries and compares their outputs directly. Since `gsync` produces the C library's capitalized error message and my implementation produces Go's lowercase version, I need to account for this mismatch. The regex normalization approach handles the binary name but not the error text itself, so I need to figure out how the other tests handle this discrepancy. I'm settling on discarding stderr for the test cases since that's what other utilities in the codebase do. Now I need to focus on implementing `cmd/sync/main.go` first to meet the core requirement, then decide whether to add tests. Let me verify the implementation matches gsync's behavior by checking how similar commands handle error message formatting.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2192,"cache_read_input_tokens":44527,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2192},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"32d25cb8-d0bd-458b-866d-1aa82465e655","uuid":"120b615d-4f4f-4