{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QQFi5Hk4itada1d2u5o4n5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The error message capitalization differs. Go's syscall errors are lowercase, C library's strerror() is uppercase. I have two options:\n\n1. Capitalize the first letter of the error message from the syscall\n2. Use a normalizer in tests that normalizes case\n\nLooking at the existing codebase, `md5sum` and `head` tests discard stderr entirely. The `sort` test uses regex to normalize the binary name. Neither fixes capitalization.\n\nLet me see what touch does for its error messages and if there's a common pattern for capitalizing syscall errors.\n\nActually, looking at this more practically: in the test, I should use a normalizer that handles both the binary name difference and the case difference. The simplest approach is discardStderr for error test cases, which is what md5sum and head do.\n\n\nFor the main.go itself, the error capitalization is just a cosmetic difference in stderr. The test framework handles this through normalizers. I think the cleanest solution is to capitalize the first letter of the OS error message in the sysMsg function to match what the C library produces, rather than relying on test normalizers.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":958,"cache_read_input_tokens":46719,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":958},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"32d25cb8-d0bd-458b-866d-1aa82465e655","uuid":"69c84845-03c1-4149-9f65-d02c471a898e"}
